{
  "sim_type": "excel",
  "title": "Return Ratios & Capital Efficiency: TCS vs Titan Stock Analysis",
  "scenario": "You are a Mumbai equity desk analyst. Your PM has asked you to compare TCS (IT services, high margins, asset-light) and Titan (retail jewellery, thin margins, capital-intensive) across five years of financials. She wants to understand why one might create value despite lower margins, using return ratios and cost-of-capital benchmarks.",
  "instructions": "Calculate ROE, ROA, ROCE, and ROIC for both companies across five years; compute the spread (return minus cost of capital) for each; identify which business demonstrates superior value creation despite differing margin profiles.",
  "learning_objective": "Compute ROE, ROA, ROCE, and ROIC for real Indian companies; benchmark returns against cost of capital; identify which business creates genuine shareholder value and justify the finding independent of margin size.",
  "hints": [
    "ROE = Net Profit ÷ Equity; ROA = Net Profit ÷ Total Assets; ROCE = EBIT ÷ (Equity + Debt); check the Data sheet for each company's profit, assets, equity, and debt rows.",
    "ROIC = NOPAT ÷ Invested Capital, where NOPAT = EBIT × (1 − tax rate). Use the tax rate row to compute after-tax operating profit. Cost of capital (WACC) is given in the Data sheet for each company.",
    "The spread = Return − Cost of Capital. A positive spread means value creation. Sum the five-year spreads for ROCE and ROIC to see which company creates more cumulative value. ROCE is financing-neutral, so it reveals true business quality."
  ],
